<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="grading230" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Grading Information</title>

  <introduction><p>The following includes descriptions of the graded components of the course and the grading scale.</p>
  </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="G_homework"><title>Homework</title>
      <p>Homework will be due once a week on Fridays at 3 pm. Assignments are posted on the course website, accessible through 
      Blackboard. Homework will be graded using a holistic grading system. Each section will be scored 0, 5 or 10. You will get a 
      5 for sets that are mostly complete, but missing some fundamental understanding. You will get a score of 10 if it is 
      complete and mostly correct. You are permitted to not do problems that you feel 100% confident that you could do correctly 
      if asked. Just indicate that it is a problem you are confident in, along with a brief reason. However, be prepared that 
      I may ask you to explain it at a later date. It is generally not advisable to skip the proofs. I will provide feedback on 
      all homework and any problems may be resubmitted to improve your score. All rewrites are due one week following the original 
      due date. In addition, with each Homework set you can earn 4 points for answering the following reflection questions:
        <ol>
          <li><p>What is one problem or concept you feel you are still struggling with or feel the least sure about after completing this section?</p></li>
          <li><p>What is one problem or concept that you feel you understand better after completing this section?</p></li>
          <li><p>Did you get any help from anyone on this section? Describe the help you got.</p></li>
          <li><p>Did you help anyone else with this homework? Describe the help you gave.</p></li>
        </ol></p>

      <p>Homework extensions will be granted upon request. However, if a student has more than 3 overdue sections, 
      they will receive a 0 in the most over-due sections. It is imperative that students stay caught up on the homework in 
      order to keep up with new material.
      </p>
      <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>How to turn in? Written assignments, generally not accepted electronically.</p></li>
        <li><p>Late submission accepted? Yes. If no more than 3 missing sections.</p></li>
        <li><p>Corrections allowed? Yes. Accepted within 1 week of original due date.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="G_activities"><title>In Class Activites</title>
      <p>Each section will have several activities that you will work on as a class or in small groups.  
      All of the Activities should be done in the text on <url href="https://landing.runestone.academy/" 
      visual="https://landing.runestone.academy/">Runestone Academy</url>. You will not be assessed on your specific answers 
      to the activities, but the Activities and Check Your Understanding questions will be graded for completion. The Activities 
      and Check Your Understanding questions will be due every Tuesday by 11:30 pm. These may be completed up to one week late. 
      Generally you should complete the majority of this work in class. You are encouraged to have a laptop or tablet for 
      submitting your work in class.</p>
      <p><ul>
        <li><p>How to turn in? Completed in the textbook on Runestone.</p></li>
        <li><p>Late submission accepted? Yes, within 1 week.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
      
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="G_DQ"><title>Daily Questions</title>
    <p> At the start of class almost every day you will be asked to answer (and turn in your answer to) the Daily Questions. 
    These will be a questions on previous material. The purpose of these is primarily diagnostic, not evaluative. They will be 
    graded on a pass/ no pass (P/NP) basis. You may receive a NP on five Daily Questions with no penalty, after that, your will 
    lose 3% of the Daily Question grade for each NP. There are absolutely no make-up questions. Excused absences arranged ahead 
    of class will not count as NP; otherwise, missed Daily Question counts as a NP. It is important to arrive to class on time 
    as these will only take a few minutes.</p></subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="quizzes"><title>Group Oral Assessment of Learning (GOAL)</title>
      <p>There will four group oral midterm quizzes. The class will be split into two groups. The quiz will be done in-class. 
      The problems may be solved and presented by any subset of your group. The entire group will get the same grade, but students 
      who contribute more may earn an additional 3% while students who are not active participants may lose 3%.  It is expected 
      that you study for the quiz, ideally as a group. Any student who is unable to attend class, for whatever reason, will have an 
      opportunity to take an alternative oral quiz outside of class time. The purpose of these is to encourage teamwork and 
      communication, both of which are important skills in mathematics. </p>

      <p>
      <dl>
        <li><title>GOAL 1</title><p> Friday, Sept. 25. Covers Ch 1-3.</p></li>
        <li><title>GOAL 2</title><p> Friday, Oct. 30. Covers Ch 4-5.</p></li>
        <li><title>GOAL 3</title><p> Tuesday, Nov. 17. Covers Ch 6-8.</p></li>
        <li><title>GOAL 4</title><p> Wednesday, Dec. 16. Covers Ch 9-10.</p></li>
      </dl>
      </p>


      <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>What if I miss a GOAL? You can schedule an individual quiz outside of class time.</p></li>
        
      </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>


